\DOC LOCAL_ADD_TWICE4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_ADD_TWICE4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic {eventually} execution goal across one inlined
modular-addition subroutine, replacing the current state variable with a
successor state and recording the new buffer contents together with an
arithmetic characterisation of the result.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes an {int} — in all observed traces the same fixed value —
selecting an offset/variant used to locate the code segment being stepped over,
and a {string list} of three names giving the destination variable and the two
operand variables of the addition; these names are those that appear in the new
assumptions and in the updated memory-read assumption. Applied to a goal whose
conclusion is an {eventually} statement about a state {s}, it produces one goal
about a fresh successor state variable {s'}:
{
   A ?- eventually ... s
   ================================================ LOCAL_ADD_TWICE4_TAC n [dest; op1; op2]
   A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent Here the conclusion retains the same postcondition body with only the
state argument updated. In {A'}, every assumption mentioning the old state —
the program-counter read, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame — is discharged and re-added in the same form for
{s'}, with the program counter advanced, the {MAYCHANGE} frame possibly
extended with a further memory component, and the memory read for the
destination buffer now equated to the newly named result value. Purely
mathematical assumptions that do not mention the state are kept unchanged, and
one new assumption is added stating that the new result is congruent to the sum
of the two operands modulo the prime, sometimes guarded by a bound hypothesis
on an operand.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show exactly this single-goal state-advancing behaviour on
goals of this one shape.

\ENDDOC
